00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.890
Google App Script
to Automate G Suite,

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.960
then stay tuned, because
here's the recap.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.166
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.300
App Script is a serverless
JavaScript runtime

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.784
for G Suite automation,
extension, and integration.

00:00:22.784 --> 00:00:24.200
COLT MCANLIS: Which
is a fancy way

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.480
of saying that your script
runs when you need it to

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.882
without you needing to provision
servers for it to run on.

00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:32.090
RETO MEIER: App Script code
is written in JavaScript,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.280
and it runs within a
sandboxed interpreter that

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.700
provides access to
our APIs and services.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.450
COLT MCANLIS: Most
importantly, App Script

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.240
makes it extremely easy
to enhance G Suite to meet

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.380
your business needs.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.300
For example, you
can use App Script

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.670
to help you do things like
automate document approval,

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.340
simplify and create
dashboards, user provisioning,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.960
and pretty much
everything in between.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.210
RETO MEIER: There are a
number of entry points for you

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.980
to start using App Script
within your organization.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.660
The first is Docs, Sheets,
and the Forms script editor.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.210
We call scripts created this
way container-bound scripts,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.160
or just bound scripts.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.170
You can easily create
simple trigger functions

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.060
that respond to changes
on the document.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.790
For example, on Open or on Edit.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.160
Within Sheets, you can
create custom functions

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.050
that work just like
the built-in functions,

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.502
but are implemented as
JavaScript in your script.

00:01:18.502 --> 00:01:20.210
Now, if you are looking
for a script that

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.530
attaches to a document
like a bound script

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.130
but could be attached to
many different documents,

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.290
you're probably
looking for an add-on.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.790
COLT MCANLIS: The
second entry point

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:32.090
is through the cloud IDE
at script.google.com.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.680
This is ideal for
scripts that aren't

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.007
tied to a particular document.

00:01:35.007 --> 00:01:37.340
That could include anything
from one-off utility scripts

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.350
to web app scripts and add-ons.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.120
You can think of
these as bound scripts

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.680
that can be bound
to any document

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.429
by any user who installs it.

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:46.220
RETO MEIER: Now, the
final two entry points

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.870
are a little bit
more specialized.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.360
You can build AdWord scripts
to automate your AdWords

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.840
campaigns, or use scripts within
App Maker, which is currently

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.156
still in beta.

00:01:55.156 --> 00:01:57.530
COLT MCANLIS: In each case,
the built-in App Script's API

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.800
provides access to
other Google or GCP APIs

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.260
as advance services,
simplified AJAX,

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.735
and Stackdriver integration.

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:06.860
RETO MEIER: If you'd like
to know more about what's

